To add bearing friction, consider in which cases the bearings are active. There turn out to be two cases: x motion and y motion. Therefore, it is easier to work with an effective x friction and an effective y friction.
The linear generate friction such that F = -mu_l v. 
Rolling bearings generally generate friction such that F = -mu_r theta'. We convert this to a form using rolling velocity: F = -mu_rv v:
F = -mu_r theta' = -mu_rv r_b theta' = -mu_rv v. With r_b the bearing radius.
-> mu_r = mu_rv r_b -> mu_rv = mu_r / r_b

The rolling bearings and linear bearings can now be added up to an effective x friction coefficient and y friction coefficient:
In the case of x translation, we have two active linear x-bearings and 8 active rolling bearings:
mu_x = 2*mu_lx + 8*mu_r/r_b
= 2 mu_lx + 8 mu_rv

In the case of y translation, we have two active linear y-bearings and 4 active rolling bearings:
mu_y = 2*mu_ly + 4*mu_r/rb
= 2 mu_ly + 4 mu_rv

=======================================================
These frictions can most easily be added to the decoupled x and y dynamics:

First, realize how friction F results in acceleration.
Our rotational inertia was (2J_m + m r_m^2).
Therefore, the linear inertia is (2J_m / r_m^2 + m)          (mr^2=I -> m=I/r^2)

F = (2J_m / r_m^2 + m) a
a = F / (2J_m / r_m^2 + m)

Add this acceleration to x_x = [x', x, i_x]^T:

x_x' = [x'', x', i_x']^T
with x'' = r_m K_m i_x / (2J_m + m r_m^2) - mu_x x' / (2J_m / r_m^2 + m)
     x' = x'

     i_x' = (u_x - R_m i_x) / L_m

x_x' = f_x(x_x, u_x) = A_x x_x + B_x u_x
     
with A_x = [[-mu_x / (2J_m / r_m^2 + m),     0,     r_m K_m / (2J_m + m r_m^2)],
            [1,                              0,                   0],
            [0,                              0,              -R_m / L_m]]

     B_x = [0, 0, 1/L_m]^T


Add this acceleration to x_y = [y', y, i_y]^T:

x_y' = [y'', y', i_y']^T
with y'' = r_m K_m i_y / (2J_m + m r_m^2) - mu_y y' / (2J_m / r_m^2 + m)
     y' = y'
     i_y' = (u_y - R_m i_y) / L_m

x_y' = f_y(x_y, u_y) = A_y x_y + B_y u_y

with A_y = [[-mu_y / (2J_m / r_m^2 + m),     0,     r_m K_m / (2J_m + m r_m^2)],
            [1,                              0,                  0],
            [0,                              0,             -R_m / L_m]]

     B_y = B_x = [0, 0, 1/L_m]^T

===========================================================

Generate inputs from trajectories

To reverse the problem and derive inputs from trajectories, take our state-space equations for x:
x'' = r_m K_m i_x / (2J_m + m r_m^2) - mu_x x' / (2J_m / r_m^2 + m)
i_x' = (u_x - R_m i_x) / L_m
->
i_x = ((2J_m + m r_m^2) x'' + mu_x r_m^2 x') / (r_m K_m)
u_x = L_m i_x' + R_m i_x
->
i_x' = ((2J_m + m r_m^2) x''' + mu_x r_m^2 x'') / (r_m K_m)

Now substitute i_x' and i_x into the equation for u_x:

u_x = L_m ((2J_m + m r_m^2) x''' + mu_x r_m^2 x'') / (r_m K_m) + R_m ((2J_m + m r_m^2) x'' + mu_x r_m^2 x') / (r_m K_m)
= (L_m (2J_m + m r_m^2) x''' + (L_m mu_x r_m^2 + R_m (2J_m + m r_m^2)) x'' + R_m mu_x r_m^2 x') / (r_m K_m)

Similarly for y:
y'' = r_m K_m i_y / (2J_m + m r_m^2) - mu_y y' / (2J_m / r_m^2 + m)
i_y' = (u_y - R_m i_y) / L_m
->
i_y = ((2J_m + m r_m^2) y'' + mu_y r_m^2 y') / (r_m K_m)
u_y = L_m i_y' + R_m i_y
->
i_y' = ((2J_m + m r_m^2) y''' + mu_y r_m^2 y'') / (r_m K_m)
->
u_y = (L_m (2J_m + m r_m^2) y''' + (L_m mu_y r_m^2 + R_m (2J_m + m r_m^2)) y'' + R_m mu_y r_m^2 y') / (r_m K_m)

Therefore, from x''', x'', x', y''', y'', y', we can calculate u_x and u_y.

